+++ b/tests/model_reference/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# Pinned SGLang model reference
+
+This test compares the native text generator with SGLang `f04d886bf79aa3d6237cbb12a3311838eb9f9bd8`, using the Qwen3.8-27B-FP8 snapshot `c3a462cc3d38cf351f3b26cbd763469eeb74ef81`. It uses greedy target-only generation on one B200. The fixture manifests pin the prompts, teacher histories, full vocabulary logits and acceptance criteria. The test tools are outside the deployed binaries.
+
+`archive-manifest.json` binds five existing SGLang captures. It includes a 128-token page edge, 897- and 2017-token content prompts, and 2047- and 2048-token prompts. The full capture recipe adds one input token, the 127- and 129-token page edges, new prose, Python, Japanese and mixed Unicode, and checkpoint EOS. The full run uses a separately verified and pinned `manifest.json`. Logit row 0 predicts the token after the prompt. Row `i` predicts the token after the prompt and the first `i` teacher tokens.
+
+## Run with frozen evidence
+
+Set `bundle` to the unpacked reference bundle, `build` to the native XMake build directory, `checkpoint` to the pinned model directory, and `out` to a new evidence location. Use the project Pixi C++/CUDA environment for the interposer, and Python with NumPy for the checker. Pass an already leased B200 as the sole device in `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`.
+
+```sh
+suite=$PWD/tests/model_reference
+toolkit="$CONDA_PREFIX/targets/x86_64-linux"
+mkdir -p "$out"
+"$CXX" -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -shared -fPIC \
+  -I"$toolkit/include" "$suite/teacher.cpp" -ldl -o "$out/teacher.so"
+OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python -B "$suite/compare.py" "$bundle" --self-test
+OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python -B "$suite/compare.py" "$bundle" \
+  --generate "$build/bin/sglang-generate" --checkpoint "$checkpoint" \
+  --teacher "$out/teacher.so" --output "$out/generate" --prefill exact
+```
+
+The self-test verifies the frozen bundle and rejects the real W1 next-position row substituted for row 0 and the W1 score vector with token IDs rotated. `--case W1 --case H2047` selects a bounded smoke test. `--prefill tensor-core` covers that policy separately. A gate failure exits with status 1 and keeps the measurements.
+
+The regular CLI returns all 248,320 logits via `--top-logits` and emits the native greedy selection. The test interposer identifies the winner readback followed by the full BF16 score readback on the same stream. It saves the original greedy ID and changes the host and device winner after the score copy, allowing normal native decode to consume the external teacher token. The checker restores vocabulary order from the JSON IDs and verifies the recorded original winner. A pass-through control must preserve every observed score. A changed teacher token must preserve the score before selection and change the following row. Neither control supplies expected model logits.
+
+## Run the HTTP widths
+
+The test exercises normal streaming completion at widths 1, 2 and 4 on the published binary and adds width 3 with graph scheduling on the staged binary. Run each binary against the same external fixture. With a full bundle the width-4 burst uses three distinct prompts and repeats the prose prompt once. Pass `--archive-smoke` with the archive bundle to run the width checks on copies of W1. It checks tokens against all available SGLang score rows while the prefixes agree. An archived position without scores requires the recorded SGLang token so that it cannot silently skip later checks. The batch logs must show overlapping decode twice at the requested width. The existing batch graph release tests provide separate replay instrumentation.
+
+```sh
+PYTHONPATH="$PWD/tests/batch" OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python -B "$suite/serve.py" \
+  "$published_build/bin/sglang-server" "$checkpoint" "$bundle" "$out/published-http" \
+  --widths 1 2 4
+PYTHONPATH="$PWD/tests/batch" OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python -B "$suite/serve.py" \
+  "$graph_build/bin/sglang-server" "$checkpoint" "$bundle" "$out/graph-http" \
+  --widths 1 2 3 4 --graph
+```
+
+HTTP token checks use the SGLang score row for the shared prefix. A close alternate requires a top-16 position and at most 1.0 logit of regret. Later tokens after a divergence have a different history and are omitted from numerical checks. The checker reports where comparison ended. Native HTTP accepts `ignore_eos=true` and explicit `stop_token_ids`. The EOS case configures the observed checkpoint EOS as that stop token and requires the SGLang token sequence and stop reason.
+
+## Recreate or audit the fixture
+
+`archive` is the original `fp8-projection-parity/evidence/sglang-v1` evidence directory. It has `fixtures/*.prompt`, `fixtures/*.teacher`, `*.f32` and `*.json`. `fixtures.py` derives the edge inputs from W1 and tokenizes the fixed holdout texts with the pinned checkpoint tokenizer.
+
+```sh
+python -B "$suite/fixtures.py" "$checkpoint" "$archive" "$out/inputs.json"
+python -B "$suite/bundle.py" "$out/inputs.json" "$archive" "$out/archive-bundle"
+```
+
+For an optional heavyweight independent replay, use the existing isolated SGLang-compatible environment and dependency overlay from the projection study. The input `source_archive` is the pinned `vision-parity/upstream/sglang.tar.gz`. Extract its `*/python/*` members into a fresh directory and set `sglang_python` to that extracted `python` directory. `checkpoint_manifest` is the authenticated `checkpoint.sha256` from the pinned reference environment. Use offline mode, a leased device and a writable evidence cache for CUDA, Triton and FlashInfer. Use a short writable `TMPDIR` so the generated ZeroMQ Unix socket paths fit in 107 bytes. The capture verifies the pinned source directory, the checkpoint manifest and checkpoint files before launch.
+
+```sh
+export MODEL_REFERENCE_SGLANG_PYTHON="$sglang_python"
+export PYTHONPATH="$reference_overlay:$sglang_python"
+export PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1
+"$reference_python" -B "$suite/reference.py" \
+  "$checkpoint" "$checkpoint_manifest" "$source_archive" \
+  "$out/inputs.json" "$out/capture"
+python -B "$suite/bundle.py" "$out/inputs.json" "$archive" "$out/full-bundle" \
+  --capture "$out/capture"
+```
+
+The optional capture makes two runs of each new request within one independent engine session and records raw FP32 rows before teacher selection. `--all` also recaptures the five archived cases for drift investigation. Bundle resynthesis retains the original archived vectors and compares expected digests byte for byte. A replay that changes a digest remains separate evidence. If the suite has no pinned full manifest yet, the bundler writes a candidate manifest and exits with an error. Review the independent capture receipts and reproducibility, then pin the verified manifest alongside `archive-manifest.json` before running native against the new cases.
+
+## Numerical criteria
+
+Every aligned row reports raw relative L2, cosine similarity, top-16 intersection, both winning IDs and margins, and reference regret. Compute norms in FP64. The row limits are 0.15 relative L2, 0.985 cosine and at least eight common top-16 IDs. A reference margin of at least 1.0 requires the same greedy winner. Where a second reference capture exists, both must agree on the winner and clear that minimum before applying the winner gate. Archive margins are single-capture observations. These are release-suite test criteria for the mixed FP8 model on this hardware.
+
+The prior fixed-model projection study observed 0.1252 relative L2 for W2 row 0 and 0.1132 for H2047 row 0. A 0.15 ceiling allows modest headroom above that calibration while rejecting larger drift. One logit is well above the observed BF16 rounding increments near the top of those rows. The published H2047 disagreement was 2.875 logits by SGLang's margin and is expected to remain visible. SGLang uses BF16 KV and a FlashInfer groupwise FP8 projection backend, while native uses MXFP8 KV and its own activation quantization and accumulation. The model combines 48 recurrent GDN and 16 GQA blocks. Cross-engine score and token deviations alone cannot isolate a native implementation defect. A regression claim requires a controlled comparison with the same history and the relevant arithmetic or execution path held fixed.
